X('dangerous-goods-classes-explained',[
 ('The nine classes with examples',[('table',['Class','Hazard','Examples'],[['1','Explosives','Fireworks, ammunition'],['2','Gases','Aerosols, LPG, oxygen cylinders'],['3','Flammable liquids','Paint, solvents, perfume, petrol'],['4','Flammable solids','Matches, some metal powders'],['5','Oxidisers, organic peroxides','Bleaching agents, hardeners'],['6','Toxic and infectious','Pesticides, lab samples'],['7','Radioactive','Medical isotopes'],['8','Corrosives','Acids, some batteries'],['9','Miscellaneous','Lithium batteries, dry ice']],'Some goods have more than one hazard, shown as a subsidiary risk.')]),
 ('Packing groups',['Within many classes, the packing group shows the degree of danger: group I is high, II medium and III low. The packing group decides the type of packaging allowed and the quantity limits. A product such as a paint can be group II or III, depending on its flash point and boiling point, so the group comes from the safety data sheet, not from guesswork.']),
 ('Before you book',['Identify the UN number, the class and the packing group from the safety data sheet.','Use packaging that is certified for the class and group, with the right marks and labels.','Tell your forwarder that the cargo is dangerous when you ask for the quote. Many lines and airlines accept only some classes, and the price and the booking time differ from general cargo.'])],
 [('Do small quantities need to be declared?','Often yes. There are limited and excepted quantity rules that reduce the requirements for small amounts, but they have conditions, and the goods must still be identified. Ask your forwarder before you assume an exemption.')])
X('imdg-vs-iata-dgr-dangerous-goods',[
 ('Which rules apply to your shipment',['Sea shipments follow the IMDG Code, issued by the International Maritime Organization. Air shipments follow the IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations, which are based on the technical instructions of the International Civil Aviation Organization. Road and rail have their own rules in each country or region, and multimodal shipments must meet the rules for every leg.','The classification and the UN number are the same across modes, but packing limits, marking and paperwork can differ.']),
 ('A product that behaves differently',['A lithium battery shipped by sea can be allowed in a larger quantity per package than by air, where the limits are tight and some types are forbidden on passenger aircraft. A liquid in a small inner packaging may be allowed by air only as a limited quantity, but may travel in larger amounts by sea. Always check the rules for your actual mode.']),
 ('Practical steps',['Use a shipper or forwarder with a trained dangerous goods specialist, because both codes are updated on a regular cycle.','Keep the declaration, the packaging certificates and the training records of the person who signs.','Do not reuse a document from one mode for the other without checking it.'])],
 [('Can the same declaration be used for sea and air?','Not automatically. The forms and the requirements differ, so the declaration is prepared for the mode and the carrier used.')])
X('un-numbers-and-proper-shipping-names',[
 ('How to find the right entry',['The dangerous goods lists in the IMDG Code and the IATA DGR give each substance a four-digit UN number and a proper shipping name. The safety data sheet shows the number in section 14, but it should be checked against the current code, since the sheet may be out of date.','Where a substance is not named, a generic entry applies, such as "Flammable liquid, not otherwise specified", and the technical name of the main hazardous component must be added in brackets.']),
 ('Where it must appear',['On the shipper\'s declaration for dangerous goods.','On the packages, as marks next to the hazard labels.','On the booking, the bill of lading or the air waybill, and in the stowage plan the carrier keeps.']),
 ('Matching it everywhere',['A UN number that differs between the declaration, the package and the booking is a frequent reason for refusal at the terminal. Fill the details once, from a verified source, and copy them exactly. If you change the formulation, check whether the UN number changes too.'])],
 [('Is the UN number the same as the HS code?','No. The UN number identifies the dangerous substance for transport rules. The HS code classifies the goods for customs. A product has both.')])
X('safety-data-sheet-sds-explained',[
 ('The sections that matter for shipping',['Section 2 gives the hazards, and section 9 the physical properties such as flash point and boiling point, which decide the class and the packing group. Section 14 gives transport information with the UN number, class and packing group. Section 7 covers handling and storage, and section 10 shows what the goods react with, which matters when you load different cargo together.','The sheet is written by the manufacturer under the rules of the country where it is made, and the other rules may differ.']),
 ('Using it properly',['Ask the supplier for the latest version, and check the date. An old sheet may carry outdated information.','If the product is a mixture, ask for the sheet for that exact product and not for a similar one.','Check section 14 against the current IMDG Code or IATA DGR before you rely on it.']),
 ('What it cannot do',['It is not a transport document. The carrier needs the shipper\'s declaration and the correct marks and labels, and the sheet only supports them. If the sheet and the product differ, the declaration is the shipper\'s responsibility.'])],
 [('How many sections does an SDS have?','Sixteen, in a standard order. Section 14 covers transport information, and sections 2 and 9 are the base for the classification.')])
X('lithium-battery-shipping-guide',[
 ('Which number applies',['UN 3480 is for lithium-ion batteries shipped alone, and UN 3481 for those packed with or contained in equipment. UN 3090 and UN 3091 apply to lithium metal batteries in the same way. The class is 9, and the limits depend on the watt-hour rating for lithium-ion and the lithium content for lithium metal.','Batteries must also be proven safe through the UN test summary, which the manufacturer provides.']),
 ('Typical rules in practice',['Batteries must be protected against short circuit, with the terminals covered and the batteries packed so they cannot move.','There are limits on the state of charge for lithium-ion batteries shipped alone by air, and on the number of batteries per package.','Marks and labels are needed, and a shipper\'s declaration is needed for most packages above the exempted limits.']),
 ('Special cases',['Damaged, defective or recalled batteries have strict rules, and are often forbidden by air. Do not ship them without advice from a dangerous goods specialist.','Some airlines and lines add their own restrictions, so check before booking.','Power banks and spare batteries are batteries too, and are covered by the same rules.'])],
 [('Can I ship lithium batteries by post or courier?','Many couriers accept only certain batteries under their own rules, and the national postal rules are strict. Ask the carrier, and never conceal a battery in the description.')])
X('wood-packaging-ispm-15',[
 ('What the stamp tells you',['The ISPM 15 mark shows the IPPC symbol, the country code, the producer or treater number and the treatment code: HT for heat treatment or MB for methyl bromide. The wood must also be free of bark. A wooden pallet or crate with this mark can usually enter other countries without a separate certificate for the wood.','Treatment is done by an approved company, and the stamp must be on two opposite sides of the item.']),
 ('What does not need treatment',['Wood packaging made fully of processed wood such as plywood, OSB or particle board is exempt, as are thin wood pieces and some other forms under the standard. The rules of the destination decide, so confirm with the buyer or the forwarder.']),
 ('If a shipment is not compliant',['The importing country can order the wood to be treated, destroyed or the cargo returned, at the shipper\'s cost. Delays and storage fees follow. For a regular exporter, buy pallets from a supplier that provides a proper ISPM 15 mark and a delivery document, and keep the receipt.'])],
 [('Do plastic and metal pallets need ISPM 15?','No. The standard applies to wood packaging material. Plastic, metal and processed wood pallets are exempt.')])
X('fumigation-certificate-for-exports',[
 ('How the process runs',['An approved fumigation company treats the cargo or the container with a fumigant, in a controlled way and for the time that is set, and then issues a certificate that records the chemical, the dose, the exposure time and the date. The certificate is attached to the export documents and is shown to the authority at the destination, if it asks.','The treatment is usually done before the container is closed, or in a place close to the port.']),
 ('Safety and timing',['Fumigation uses toxic gas, so the cargo area must be cleared and the gas released properly. The container or cargo may need to be ventilated before it is opened, and the time can be up to a day or more.','Book the service well before the cut-off, because a missed slot can mean a missed vessel. Ask the company about the time and the cost.']),
 ('Check the buyer\'s needs',['Some destinations require fumigation of particular cargo, such as grains, wood or some agricultural produce, and some countries do not accept certain fumigants. The requirement comes from the buyer\'s country, so ask for the rules in writing, and do not assume that a certificate from a past shipment will do.'])],
 [('Is a fumigation certificate the same as a phytosanitary certificate?','No. A fumigation certificate records the treatment. A phytosanitary certificate is issued by the plant protection authority and states that plants or plant products meet the import rules.')])
X('project-cargo-and-break-bulk-guide',[
 ('What the early survey covers',['A good plan starts with the dimensions, the weight, the centre of gravity and the lifting points of each piece, and a route survey of the road from the factory to the port and from the port to the site. Bridges, power lines, turns and weight limits decide whether a piece can travel and what permits are needed.','Many problems are found at this stage, long before the cargo leaves, and are cheaper to solve then.']),
 ('Execution',['Heavy lift and over-dimensional cargo needs the right vessel and the right equipment at both ends. Check the crane capacity and the quay strength at the load and discharge ports, and choose between a break-bulk vessel, a heavy lift vessel and a container or flat rack for pieces that fit.','Lashing and securing must follow a plan, and some cargo needs a marine surveyor to witness the loading.']),
 ('Planning for time and cost',['Project cargo usually has long lead times, so start discussions early. Allow time for permits, for the vessel to be found and for the customs clearance. Weather and port delays can move the dates, and the contract should say how this is handled. Insurance for the full value is advised, since a single piece can be very valuable.'])],
 [('What is break-bulk cargo?','Cargo that is too large, heavy or oddly shaped for a container, and is loaded piece by piece on to a vessel, often with cranes and special lashing.')])
X('pharma-cargo-shipping-guide',[
 ('Common temperature ranges',['Pharmaceutical products are shipped within a controlled range set by the manufacturer, commonly 2 to 8 degrees Celsius for chilled products, 15 to 25 for controlled room temperature, and frozen at minus 20 or lower for some products. Some vaccines and biologics need much lower temperatures with dry ice or special containers.','The range is part of the product\'s registration, so the shipper must follow it, not the nearest general standard.']),
 ('Choosing the packaging and lane',['Active containers have their own power and temperature control, and passive shippers use insulation and cooling packs for a set time. Pick the type that matches the transit time, including the possible delay at transfers and customs. Ask the airline or line for a qualified lane, which has a record of holding the temperature.','Use data loggers in the cargo to record the temperature for the full journey.']),
 ('Paperwork and clearance',['Import and export permissions, such as a drug licence, a certificate or a sample permission, must be in place before the cargo moves.','Clear the cargo quickly at both ends. Plan for the cargo to be received by a cold store and not left in a general area.','Share the plan and the contacts with the consignee, and agree who acts if the temperature goes out of range.'])],
 [('What is good distribution practice?','It is a quality standard for storing and moving medicines so their quality is kept. It covers temperature control, records, trained staff and procedures for deviations.')])
